course: dictionary
lesson: 15647
title: Them: the people or things already named
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Them» is a small word that stands in for people or things we have already named. It saves us from saying the same names again and again.
explain: «Maya found two shells on the beach. She put them in her pocket.» «Them» means the two shells, so we do not need to say «the two shells» a second time.
explain: Sometimes «them» stands for one person when we do not know if they are a boy or a girl: «Someone left a bag here. Ask them if it is theirs.» Casual talk shortens it to «'em», as in «give 'em here», but in school writing use the full word.
fact: «Them» is a pronoun. It takes the place of a noun that has already been named.
fact: «Them» can mean more than one person or thing, as in «The puppies are hungry, so feed them».
fact: «Between them» means the named people together: «Lily and Omar shared one orange between them.»
fact: A good check: swap the name back in. «Maya put the two shells in her pocket» still makes sense, so «them» was standing in for the shells.
fact: In school English, write «those sweets», not «them sweets», and write «give them here», not «give 'em here».
exercise: «Sam had three pencils. He lent them to Priya.» What does «them» mean? | Priya's pencil case | The school bell | Sam | *The three pencils
exercise: «Someone left a bag on the bench. Ask them if it is theirs.» What does «them» mean in this sentence? | *The person who might own the bag | The colour of the bag | The school gate | The bench
exercise: Which sentence is the school English version of «them sweets is 2 dollar each»? | Them sweets are 2 dollars each | *Those sweets are 2 dollars each | 'Em sweets cost 2 dollar | Sweets them are two dollars each
test: «The rabbits were hungry, so Ben fed them carrots.» What does «them» mean? | *The rabbits | The garden | Ben | The carrots
test: «Lily and Omar shared one orange between them.» What does «between them» mean? | Omar's friends at school | *Lily and Omar together | Two oranges in a box | Lily only
exam: Which sentence uses «them» correctly? | Them went to the park happily. | *Please give the books to them. | Please give the books to they. | Please give them books to they.
